[Music]  do  [Music]  for me in the wildlife with the animals  that you're going to encounter so that  you know what the behaviors are learning  how an animal behaves  where it's likely to be found or how to  find them is half the half of what you  need to do  the other thing is i feel very fortunate  that i  collaborate with usually world experts  who have in an area of either local  knowledge or knowledge about something  specific and can give even more in-depth  understanding into the animal behavior  the location the currents or what that  i'm going to anticipate so that i can uh  put myself in the right places but i  think one of the things that i  i look back on my early life i think it  was diving and taking pictures because i  loved it  and learning about the animals and their  behaviors allowed me to find the animals  first and you know not everything just  swims right in front of you and so  obvious like the little things can be  the most the most interesting but  learning to to find something subtle is  really wonderful and sometimes like if  you can just imagine walking down the  street you don't notice the way the  leaves blow the first time but if you  walk it over and over again all of a  sudden the scent of the road the way the  leaves move and those little rhythms  become much more interesting and so  having a little more familiarity lets  you get more comfortable to get those  more intimate moments that maybe only  you see or that a few other people who  experience it see and photograph our  periods uh  a photograph so i think that that's  to me what a lot of preparation allows  for  um all while having the understanding  that things happen like this and you  have to be ready to react and react in a  moment's notice especially when i'm  photographing other people  um  like in cuba i  i like to  talk to them a little bit before i make  photographs of them  mostly because i'm not i wasn't only  there to  just to photograph but i also wanted to  learn from their stories and learn their  experiences  and from that  it helped inform  how i wanted to take the image  where i would want to take the image if  we wanted to include some like artifacts  of theirs and such like that and a lot  of the times it was also like a  collaboration with them  of how they wanted to photograph like be  presented in the photograph  like that one of the photographs the  older couple on the steps um that's  haavo and and ld when i was  photographing them he's like i wanna you  to snap the picture when when i'm like  bringing the like the bottle up to my  lips so he's like i want you to show me  like drinking this and so a lot of times  it's this  it's a collaboration with the people of  um  kind of in their own personalities and  experiences within the image rather than  either projecting my  view or my  ideology i guess on them um  and having a little bit more of their  voice within the image  well i'm trying to document a lot of  natural behavior so i would like to have  as little  uh direct impact  on the animals in the underwater world  what's really amazing  is they have to want to come close i  mean it's a big ocean out there and they  can swim away quite easily  so in order to do this there has to be  some sort of  little momentary connection between the  animal and myself  to be able to get to a range where i can  actually make a picture that's that's uh  reasonable  and so it's a wonderful sort of feeling  to learn how to move your body to  interact in a place to be a silent  observer but also to be uh someone that  that can be in a position to actually  capture uh something that's that's  occurring a feeding behavior a mating  kind of thing a  curious  whale that wants to swim up to you that  that is interested in something  uh shark that that is is interested in  in sort of moving around you and so  those  moments uh you know are natural and  happen  uh on their own way but uh getting in  the right place or positioning oneself  for those to happen is really an art  form in and of itself and something i  also spend a fair amount of time  working on is how to be in the right  place at the right time and you always  want to be in two places and you're  never in the right place and nor do i  ever have the right lens and all that  kind of stuff and so they're all a  million reasons why things don't work  out um but every now and then you have  success and it feels really wonderful  i'm definitely trying to engage in the  environments that i'm photographing  mostly because i want to create those  relationships but also like having the  camera that i have walking around a lot  of these like small towns  um in cuba a lot of times  we would hear like chatter of like  people like like i would be known  in that little town of like that'd be  like this  person walking around with this  big contraption thing  yeah when i when i want to photograph um  i definitely want to  connect as much as i can with the people  and  create a relationship  it's really the power of observation for  me that is is what's so interesting and  i really enjoy that moment and i enjoy  it not just with the camera to take the  picture but i enjoy the power of  actually learning to look um and  watching something for a while and i  find that to be one of the most  enjoyable experiences to just sort of  sit and look  even when i'm not photographing i want i  keep on observing trying to find like  the little details that like really make  a scene  um and make like almost mental images in  my mind of like  [Music]  inspiration of like possibly making that  image later  [Music]  in terms of tips for for people i think  one thing is to  pick something that is easily accessible  so in my case um  you know i like wildlife but so if we  were thinking of animals you know i  might pick animals that are local if  it's a robin that has a nest right in  your yard a squirrel cat or something  that you're comfortable with and think  of what what do you want to say about  that animal or that plant or what would  you like it to look like and what time  of day are you photographing this  instead of having things be arbitrary  pause for a moment and think what do you  want the image to say what would you  like to tell someone about that plant  why do you like that flower that stopped  and took that picture  and then begin to think about what are  the shape the form the color the time  and what is it that you really like  about it that caused you to pause in  that way and i think as you begin to  question yourself of what you want to  say about it the images  i would encourage you to then take a few  other images and say well how can i  enhance what it is i would like to  convey about it do i want to be close to  somebody do i want to be far from it do  i want to be looking down do i want to  be looking up  and you know i think those are the  elements  that you begin to have and so instead of  it just oh i snapped this picture and i  really liked it to notice them and to  really begin to sort of ask yourself  what you want to say about it with  photography think about a topic or  an emotion or something that you're like  deeply involved with or passionate about  or  that you find yourself wanting to  express or talk about and then think of  the ways that  um  you can  showcase that within photography  and i would just end on one quick thing  golfing is easy bike riding is easy but  these people practice over and over  again and no one believes me but it's a  skill to do this stuff and you have to  practice this over and over again and i  love doing this photography and i'm sure  katarina does as well  um and so it's it's something you do  over and over again and so it's not just  that i walk up to something and i get  one time lucky on this although maybe  that does happen these are skills that  i'm working on constantly on getting  myself ready so the reflexes boom i'm  right here with the camera and i'm ready  for it boom my lights are right where i  want them you know i'm thinking i'm very  aware of those things and i think that  that practice is really critically  important in the art of photography and  storytelling at whatever subject you're  interested in photographing so my number  one tip is to find a subject that you  like photograph it  and think about what you want to say  about it  you

